<p>Counted as the desk's cause table below counts them. The {oneDay} one-day moves are filed under
<code>parser</code> by tenant, so they count in #1 and again in #3, their own problem.</p>

## The desk's cause table

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The `parser` row holds the reports read as a one-day move: the desk files them there by tenant.
The parser fix does not fix them; #3 tracks them.

## How to tell them apart

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes
  the day first. The day and the month swap: 2 March becomes 3 February. It reaches every channel
  of every tenant.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, but reads what people
  type the tenant's way, day first. People follow the hint, and the date swaps. Only reports that
  came in through the form, at a tenant on booking-v3. Fixing the parser does not fix this; the
  hint has to match what the form reads.
- **`one-day`**: the booking moves by one day, not by a swap. Tuesday becomes Monday, one client
  gets Tuesday while another in a different time zone gets Monday, an invite arrives before the
  booking was made. That looks like a time zone shift when the date is stored or sent, not a
  parser bug. The desk listed these reports in its notes, but no fix covers them.

## Why one ticket hid three

- Triage merges on the subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  "Wrong date" fits all three problems, so all three landed in one ticket. Merging on subject
  alone needs a cause check before it.
- The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant: the "tagged as"
  column shows how often it disagrees. Group by tenant, form and channel, as `causes.ts` does.
- The cause table already marked `hint` as "no ticket of its own" and footnoted the one-day
  moves. Status `known` made it read as finished.

